{"name":"Telopea","hex":"#2d2541","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Telopea","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Proteaceae – waratahs."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A suburb of Sydney in the Parramatta council area, New South Wales, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Telopea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Waratah","blocks":[{"text":"The waratah is an Australian-endemic genus of five species of large shrubs to small trees, native to the southeastern parts of Australia. The best-known species in this genus is Telopea speciosissima, which has bright red flowers and is the New South Wales (NSW) state emblem. The waratah is a member of the family Proteaceae, flowering plants (Angiosperms) distributed in the Southern Hemisphere. The key diagnostic feature of Proteaceae is the inflorescence, which is often very large, brightly coloured and showy, consisting of many small flowers densely packed into a compact head or spike. Species of waratah boast such inflorescences ranging from 6–15 cm in diameter with a basal ring of coloured bracts. The leaves are spirally arranged, 10–20 cm long and 2–3 cm broad with entire or serrated margins. The name waratah comes from the Eora Aboriginal people, the traditional owners of the Sydney region."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Telopea_speciosissima_suellen.JPG/330px-Telopea_speciosissima_suellen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waratah"},{"title":"Telopea (journal)","blocks":[{"text":"Telopea is a fully open-access, online, peer-reviewed scientific journal that rapidly publishes original research on plant systematics, with broad content that covers Australia and the Asia-Pacific region. The journal was established in 1975 and is published by the National Herbarium of New South Wales, Royal Botanic Gardens & Domain Trust. As from Volume 9, part 1, 2000, full text of papers is available electronically in pdf format. It is named for the genus Telopea, commonly known as waratahs."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telopea_(journal)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG/500px-Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG/1280px-Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG/500px-Telopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATelopea_aspera_inflorescence.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Telopea_mongaensis_at_Monga_National_Park.jpg/500px-Telopea_mongaensis_at_Monga_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Telopea_mongaensis_at_Monga_National_Park.jpg/500px-Telopea_mongaensis_at_Monga_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATelopea_mongaensis_at_Monga_National_Park.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Telopea_oreades_Errinundra_02.jpg/330px-Telopea_oreades_Errinundra_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Telopea_oreades_Errinundra_02.jpg/330px-Telopea_oreades_Errinundra_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A